course: books
lesson: 16
title: Alice in Wonderland, chapter VIII, part 2: The Queen's Croquet-Ground (a head without a body)
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Croquet with live animals is hopeless. Just as Alice gets her flamingo's neck straight, it twists round and looks up into her face so puzzled that she bursts out laughing; meanwhile her hedgehog uncurls and crawls away, and the soldier-arches keep getting up and wandering off.
explain: Everyone plays at once, quarrelling and fighting over the hedgehogs, and the Queen stamps about shouting «Off with his head!» or «Off with her head!» about once a minute. Alice starts to feel uneasy: what will happen to her when it's her turn to annoy the Queen?
explain: Then a grin appears in the air: the Cheshire Cat. Bit by bit its eyes and its head appear, and Alice tells it all her complaints. When she notices the Queen listening behind her, she cleverly changes «she's so extremely—» into «—likely to win». The King doesn't like the Cat's look; the Cat says «I'd rather not» kiss his hand, and Alice reminds him that «A cat may look at a king».
explain: The Queen solves every problem the same way: «Off with his head!» But the Cat is only a head. The executioner says you can't cut off a head without a body to cut it from; the King says anything with a head can be beheaded; the Queen threatens to execute everyone. Alice says the Cat belongs to the Duchess, so they fetch the Duchess from prison.
explain: By the time she arrives, the Cat's head has faded away completely, and the King and the executioner run wildly up and down looking for it, while everyone else goes back to the game.
fact: Alice tucks her flamingo under her arm, but it twists round and looks up at her with «such a puzzled expression that she could not help bursting out laughing».
fact: Alice soon decides it is «a very difficult game indeed»: her hedgehog unrolls itself and crawls away, the ground is full of ridges and furrows, and the soldiers who make the arches keep getting up and walking off, also because they have to leave off being arches to take the people the Queen sentences into custody (to prison).
fact: «The players all played at once without waiting for turns, quarrelling all the while»; the Queen shouts «Off with his head!» about once a minute.
fact: Alice thinks: «They're dreadfully fond of beheading people here; the great wonder is, that there's any one left alive!»
fact: The Cheshire Cat appears grin first; Alice waits until its eyes, and then at least one ear, appear before talking, because «It's no use speaking to it till its ears have come» (it could not hear her), and then the whole head appears, but no body.
fact: Alice complains: they don't play fairly, they quarrel, there seem to be no rules, and the next arch is «walking about at the other end of the ground».
fact: «How do you like the Queen?» — «Not at all... she's so extremely—» — then, seeing the Queen behind her — «—likely to win, that it's hardly worth while finishing the game.» The Queen smiles and passes on.
fact: The King says the Cat may kiss his hand; the Cat answers «I'd rather not»; the King says «Don't be impertinent» (impertinent = cheeky, rude), and gets behind Alice as he speaks.
fact: «A cat may look at a king» is an old saying: even someone unimportant has the right to look at someone grand.
fact: «The Queen had only one way of settling all difficulties, great or small. "Off with his head!"», which she says about the Cat «without even looking round».
fact: Alice hears the Queen sentence three players to be executed «for having missed their turns», though nobody takes turns at all; her flamingo has gone across the garden, «trying in a helpless sort of way to fly up into a tree», and when she brings it back both hedgehogs are out of sight, «but it doesn't matter much», she thinks, «as all the arches are gone from this side of the ground».
fact: The executioner's argument: you can't cut off a head unless there is a body to cut it off from. The King's argument: anything that has a head can be beheaded. The Queen's argument: if nothing is done at once, she'll have everybody executed.
fact: Alice says «It belongs to the Duchess: you'd better ask her about it»; the Duchess is in prison, and the executioner goes off «like an arrow» to fetch her.
fact: The Cat's head fades away while he is gone; when he comes back with the Duchess, it has entirely disappeared.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «lowkey» = a bit, quietly; «salty» = cross and bitter; «wicked» (New England, USA) = very; «bostin'» (Black Country, England) = great; «our kid» (Manchester) = my brother or sister.
exercise: What keeps going wrong with Alice's flamingo? | It twists round and looks up into her face with a puzzled look | It flies off over the wall into the Duchess's kitchen to look for pepper | It bites her hand | It falls asleep
exercise: Why does Alice burst out laughing during the game? | The flamingo looks up at her with such a puzzled expression | The Queen falls over a hedgehog | The King trips over his crown and lands in a big flower-pot | The Cat tells a joke
exercise: What does Alice's hedgehog do when she is ready to hit it? | Unrolls itself and crawls away | Rolls straight through the arch | Bites the flamingo | Turns into a pig
exercise: Why are the arches a problem? | The soldiers keep getting up and walking off | They are made of glass and keep breaking into small pieces when hit | They are too small | They are painted red
exercise: How do the players take turns? | They don't: they all play at once, quarrelling | Strictly one by one, oldest first, as the rules of croquet say they must | The Queen chooses | By drawing cards
exercise: How often does the Queen shout «Off with his head!» or «Off with her head!»? | About once a minute | Once at the very start of the game | Only when she loses | Never during the game, only at the end
exercise: «The great wonder is, that there's any one left alive!» Why does Alice think this? | Everyone here is so fond of beheading people | The flamingoes are very dangerous birds that peck players to pieces | The hedgehogs are poisonous | The garden is full of holes
exercise: What does Alice see first, «a curious appearance in the air»? | A grin | A long tail | A crown | A pair of ears
exercise: Before talking to the Cat, what does Alice wait for? | Its eyes, and then at least one ear | Its whole body down to the tip of the tail, and its whiskers | The Queen to leave | Its name
exercise: How much of the Cat appears in the end? | Only the head | The whole cat, tail and all | Only its striped tail | Just the grin
exercise: Which of these is one of Alice's complaints about the game? | There don't seem to be any rules, or nobody attends to them | It is far too easy, and she wins every single round she plays against the Queen | The hedgehogs are too small | It is raining
exercise: «She's so extremely—» Alice sees the Queen behind her. How does she finish the sentence? | «—likely to win, that it's hardly worth while finishing the game» | «—kind to everyone in the garden» | «—beautiful today» | «—busy, so I'll go home»
exercise: What does the Queen do after hearing Alice's answer? | Smiles and passes on | Shouts «Off with her head!» | Gives Alice a little prize | Asks the Cat to leave
exercise: What does the King offer the Cat? | It may kiss his hand if it likes | It may have a cup of tea with the Queen and the Knave of Hearts | It may play croquet | It may sit on the throne
exercise: How does the Cat answer the King's offer? | «I'd rather not» | «Thank you, your Majesty, I would love to» | «Off with his head!» | «We're all mad here»
exercise: «Don't be impertinent,» says the King. What does impertinent mean? | Cheeky and rude | Very important | Invisible | Hungry
exercise: «A cat may look at a king.» What does this old saying mean? | Even someone unimportant may look at someone grand | Cats are the kings of all animals, in Wonderland and at home | Kings must always look at cats | Only cats can see kings
exercise: How does the Queen settle the problem of the Cat? | «Off with his head!» — without even looking round | She invites it to play croquet | She asks the Duchess to take it home to the kitchen at once | She gives it a saucer of milk
exercise: Why did the Queen sentence three players while Alice was away? | For having missed their turns | For cheating with the hedgehogs | For laughing at the Queen | For talking to the Cat
exercise: Where has Alice's flamingo gone when she wants to use it? | Across the garden, trying to fly up into a tree | Into the big flower-pot, to hide there with the three gardeners | Back to the Queen | Into the teapot
exercise: What is the executioner's argument? | You can't cut off a head unless there is a body to cut it off from | The Cat is far too small to need an executioner of his size | The King must do it himself | Cats have nine lives
exercise: What is the King's argument? | Anything that has a head can be beheaded | Cats may never be beheaded on a Tuesday, by the oldest rule in the book | The Queen must decide | Only the Duchess can behead a cat
exercise: Whom does Alice say the Cat belongs to? | The Duchess | The Mad Hatter | The Queen of Hearts | The White Rabbit
exercise: Where is the Duchess when they send for her? | In prison | In her kitchen | At the tea-party | On the croquet-ground
exercise: What has happened to the Cat by the time the Duchess arrives? | It has entirely disappeared | It has grown a body | It has eaten a hedgehog | It has become the Queen's pet
exercise: How does the executioner go off to fetch the Duchess? | Like an arrow | Very slowly, grumbling all the way | Riding on a flamingo | Hand in hand with the King
test: Jake: «Alice was bostin' at croquet, won easy.» (bostin' = great, Black Country) Put it right. | She found it «a very difficult game indeed» and never really got to play | Right: she beat the Queen in three shots and the Queen gave her a prize | Right: her flamingo helped her win | She refused to play
test: Why can the soldiers not stay as arches? | They have to get up and take the people the Queen sentences into custody | They are tired and go home for their tea in the March Hare's house | It starts to rain | The Cat scares them away
test: Ruby, lowkey worried (a bit worried): «Alice wasn't scared at all in the game, right?» Put it right. | She felt very uneasy, as she might annoy the Queen any minute | Right: she was laughing the whole time and never thought about the Queen at all | Right: she was too busy to notice | She was angry, not scared
test: Why is it «no use speaking to it till its ears have come»? | Alice thinks it couldn't hear her without ears | Cats never speak until they have grown their whole tails back again | The Queen had forbidden talking | The grin was too loud
test: Why does Alice change «she's so extremely—» into «—likely to win»? | The Queen is right behind her, listening | The Cat tells her to be polite to the Queen in a whisper | She suddenly likes the Queen | The King asks her to
test: Is it TRUE or FALSE? «Alice tells the Queen honestly that she doesn't like her.» | False: she twists her words into a compliment once she sees the Queen | True: she says «Not at all» straight to the Queen's face, then walks off | True, and the Queen laughs | False: she never speaks about the Queen
test: The King «got behind Alice as he spoke». What does this show? | He is a bit afraid of the Cat | He wants to push Alice towards the Cat | He is hiding from the angry Queen | He is looking for his lost crown
test: What would have happened if the Cat's whole body had appeared before the Duchess came? | The executioner's argument would be gone, and the Queen's order could be carried out | The King would have invited it to play croquet with the flamingoes | The Duchess would have stayed in prison | Nothing would change
test: Who has the best argument about beheading the Cat, if you think it through? | The executioner: with no body, there is nothing to cut the head off from | The Queen: executing everybody solves everything | The King, because kings are always right | Nobody: cats can't be beheaded on Tuesdays
test: Zoe, salty (cross): «the Queen sentenced three players for cheating.» Put it right. | It was «for having missed their turns» — though nobody took turns at all | It was for laughing at the King when he tripped over his own crimson velvet cushion | It was for losing their hedgehogs | It was for talking to the Cat
test: Looking back: in chapter VI, how did the Cat vanish the last time Alice saw it? | Slowly, beginning with the end of the tail and ending with the grin | All at once, in a big puff of pepper smoke from the kitchen chimney | Head first, so only the paws were left | It walked away into the wood
test: Looking back: what did Alice think was «the most curious thing I ever saw in my life» in chapter VI? | A grin without a cat | A baby turning into a pig | A footman like a fish | A cook throwing plates
test: Looking back: where did Alice first meet the Cheshire Cat? | In the Duchess's kitchen, grinning on the hearth | At the tea-party, curled up asleep in the teapot next to the Dormouse | On the mushroom with the Caterpillar | In the White Rabbit's house
test: Looking back: Alice says «you make one quite giddy» to the Cat in chapter VI. Who says «You make me giddy» in this chapter, and to whom? | The Queen, to the gardeners who keep bowing | The King, to the Cheshire Cat when it grins at him | Alice, to the flamingo | The Duchess, to the Queen
test: Looking back: the flamingo's long, bending neck is like Alice's own neck in chapter V. Who called Alice a «Serpent!» then? | The Pigeon | The Caterpillar | The Mouse | The Duchess
test: Looking back: the Cat told Alice in chapter VI «we're all mad here». Which event in this part fits that best? | Croquet with live hedgehogs and flamingoes and walking arches | Alice tucking the flamingo under her arm | The King walking with the Queen | The executioner going to the prison
test: ★★★ The executioner says no body, no beheading; the King says any head can be beheaded. Can both be right at once about the Cat? | No: one says it can't be done, the other says it can | Yes: they are saying the same thing in different words | Yes, if the Queen agrees | No, because the Cat is the Queen's
test: ★★★ Three players are sentenced «for having missed their turns». Why is that unfair in this game? | Nobody waits for turns, so no one can know when it is their turn | Missing a turn is allowed once in croquet, so three is not too many | The Queen missed hers too | Hedgehogs take the turns
test: ★★★ Careful reading: when Alice gets her flamingo back, why does she think it «doesn't matter much» that the hedgehogs are gone? | All the arches are gone from this side of the ground anyway | The Queen has already won the game and gone back into the palace for tea | The Cat had eaten them | She had decided to stop playing
exam: Identify the argument advanced by the executioner in respect of the beheading of the Cheshire Cat. | That a head cannot be severed in the absence of a body from which to sever it | That cats are exempt by royal law | That the King must perform the act himself | That the Queen's order was not in writing
exam: It is stated that Alice, upon perceiving the Queen behind her, altered her remark. Select the response which most accurately characterises the altered remark. | That the Queen was so extremely likely to win that finishing was hardly worth while | That the Queen was the most beautiful lady she had ever seen in any garden | That she wished to go home immediately | That the Cat was impertinent
exam: Ascertain the proprietor of the Cheshire Cat as declared by Alice. | The Duchess | The King of Hearts | The March Hare | Alice's sister
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the conduct of the game of croquet? | The players played all at once, without waiting for turns, and quarrelled throughout | Each player awaited the conclusion of the preceding stroke | The Queen was defeated by Alice | No sentences were pronounced
